Overview
Today I completed a PortSwigger Web Security Academy lab focused on identifying the backend database type and version using UNION-based SQL Injection.
Challenge
Raw SQL payloads entered directly into the browser URL caused Internal Server Errors because special characters and comments were not being interpreted correctly.
Solution
I used URL encoding to preserve the structure of the SQL payload.
Payload Used
'+UNION+SELECT+@@version,+NULL%23
Key Concepts Learned
Database Version Detection
MySQL:
SELECT @@version
Microsoft SQL Server:
SELECT @@version
PostgreSQL:
SELECT version()
Oracle:
SELECT banner FROM v$version
URL Encoding
Space = + or %20
= %23
' = %27
Result
Successfully retrieved the backend MySQL version and solved the lab.
Key Insight
Sometimes the vulnerability is not the difficult part. Understanding how browsers encode URLs can be the difference between a failed payload and a successful exploit.
